      "text": "To solve this probability problem, we will follow a step-by-step approach. \n\nProbability Calculation:\n\nFirst, let's determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10 clubs:\n\n\\[ C(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\]\n\nwhere n is the total number of items, k is the number of drawn items, and ! denotes the factorial function. Here, n = 10 and k = 2.\n\n\\[ \nC(10, 2) = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45 \n\\]\n\nCalculating this, we find there are 45 different ways to choose 2 members from the 10 member club.\n\nNext, let's find out how the probability changes if only one member is a girl:\n\nIf one person is chosen as a girl, the other necessarily needs to be another girl in order to meet the point requirement. We calculate how many ways we can potentially find a girl at birth:\n\nThere are 5 girls out of the 10 members:\n\n\\[ C(5, 1) = \\frac{5!}{1!(5-1)!} = \\frac{5}{1} = 5 \\]\n\nHaving calculated the scenarios where both girls are chosen, the prob:\n\n\n---\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{10}}\\).",
